  "notes": "The melodic-sad-trap loop bench. Two things are specific enough to author directly. The first is the harmony: the research writes out i–VI–III–VII *and* i–VII–VI–V, and the second of those is the mournful one — a descending line resolving through a real dominant — so `borrowed.V` is raised to 0.25 and both carry equal top weight, with 7th/9th extensions outweighing triads. The \"occasional relative-major lift on the last bar\" is the i–iv–VII–III family, which ends on III. The second is the arrangement: the research says drum drop-outs are *the main dynamic device*, not one of several, so `dropOutBeats.prob` is 0.85 — the highest in this batch — and the bridge drops the drums entirely while the verse stays at a fixed 16 bars. Melody sits low and consonant (64–88, `chordToneBias` 0.75) because it is a piano/guitar loop rather than a lead over a beat. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium. The inferred parameters are the drum numbers, which the paragraph gives only as \"moderate\", and the melodic register. ⚠ The loop-trading workflow the research describes (co-produced loops assembled with other loopmakers) is not representable as a generator parameter and is deliberately not encoded — it would have to be faked as extra progression families, which is the dishonest fix.",
